WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.080 --> 00:00:04.320
One of the stories we've been talking about&nbsp;
now, really for the last five years since the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:04.320 --> 00:00:09.440
Trump era started, but even in particular&nbsp;
in the context of the January 6th riots,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:09.440 --> 00:00:14.960
which are sort of like the culmination&nbsp;
of a peak of complete non-thinking,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:14.960 --> 00:00:20.160
anti intellectual, radical violent extremism,&nbsp;
the question we've been thinking about is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:20.160 --> 00:00:24.720
sort of like, how far back does this go? When&nbsp;
did this start to infect the American right wing?&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:24.720 --> 00:00:31.600
And there are people who wrongly believe either&nbsp;
that Donald Trump created this extremism,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:31.600 --> 00:00:39.680
and that's that's definitely not true or that the&nbsp;
extremism started in the last five to 10 years and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:39.680 --> 00:00:45.600
Donald Trump merely coalesced it, which is like&nbsp;
five percent closer to the truth, but but still&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:45.600 --> 00:00:50.240
very much not accurate. And James Rosen wrote a&nbsp;
really interesting piece in the Hartford Courant&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:50.240 --> 00:00:54.720
called Republican Embrace of Extremism&nbsp;
Began Long Before Trump. And you can&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:54.720 --> 00:00:59.840
read about it. And in the piece, James&nbsp;
Rosen actually goes back to talking about&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:00.640 --> 00:01:07.440
1994 and Newt Gingrich as a congressman at the&nbsp;
time of becoming speaker of the House and how that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:07.440 --> 00:01:13.680
was sort of a seminal moment in the radicalization&nbsp;
of the Republican Party. And I think to a great&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:13.680 --> 00:01:21.440
extent that's true. But the stage was actually set&nbsp;
even before that and would go back, I would say,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:22.160 --> 00:01:30.480
to the 1980s and Ronald Reagan. And you don't see&nbsp;
the same. I mean, listen, there's no doubt that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:30.480 --> 00:01:37.040
Lauren Bobert, Marjorie Taylor Green, Trumpy and&nbsp;
radicalism stormed the Capitol in this type of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:37.040 --> 00:01:43.360
stuff that was not around in the 80s and that&nbsp;
was not even around in the in the mid 90s when&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:43.360 --> 00:01:47.920
Newt Gingrich took over. But it's a progression.&nbsp;
And this is why I've been arguing that it's going&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:47.920 --> 00:01:54.880
to take realistically a decade, maybe decades.&nbsp;
I don't know. I mean, maybe eight to 10 years&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:54.880 --> 00:01:59.760
minimum to make a big dent in this, because it's&nbsp;
been building for so long. And we've interviewed&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:59.760 --> 00:02:03.440
over the last over the last eight years, I've&nbsp;
interviewed people on all aspects of this&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:04.240 --> 00:02:13.520
conspiracy ism, anti intellectual ism, religious&nbsp;
radicalization, cult like mentality, educational&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:13.520 --> 00:02:18.800
issues that lead to people being susceptible to&nbsp;
this stuff, lack of media literacy, all of these&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:18.800 --> 00:02:25.600
different things are sort of charted parallel to&nbsp;
the political story that is that has taken over&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:25.600 --> 00:02:30.480
on the American right. And if you really want&nbsp;
to, you could go back to the civil rights era.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:31.200 --> 00:02:42.000
You could actually go back to late 50s, 60s, civil&nbsp;
rights era and Roe v Wade era where I don't know,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:42.000 --> 00:02:47.760
I it's almost like when you get to 60 years,&nbsp;
it starts to become harder to tell the story&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:47.760 --> 00:02:53.840
in discrete steps. But you could even say&nbsp;
it started then. But certainly in the 80s,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:53.840 --> 00:03:02.720
with Reaganomics and the start of the spread of&nbsp;
in the more academic side supply side economics,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:02.720 --> 00:03:08.400
but the way that that infected many on the right&nbsp;
with these sort of faux libertarian ideas that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:08.400 --> 00:03:14.000
become very authoritarian when the outcomes are&nbsp;
not what they want. Stories about welfare queens,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:15.360 --> 00:03:20.640
free riders on insurance, social welfare&nbsp;
and insurance plans and this type of thing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:20.640 --> 00:03:26.800
in the 80s that primed the pump for&nbsp;
Newt Gingrich to come in in 1994&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:27.360 --> 00:03:33.600
and go to the next level. And Newt Gingrich in 94&nbsp;
certainly starts to make the Republican Party look&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:33.600 --> 00:03:41.760
more like the radicalism we see now. This led&nbsp;
to the impeachment of Bill Clinton and and very&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:41.760 --> 00:03:49.229
similar sort of persecution type radicalism from&nbsp;
the right. This then combined with continued anti&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:49.229 --> 00:03:55.440
intellectual ism to give us George W. Bush and&nbsp;
remember that the non-thinking right of today&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:56.400 --> 00:04:02.080
blows the non-thinking right of the Bush era&nbsp;
out of the water. But Bush was really the guy&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:02.080 --> 00:04:05.760
that started this. Oh, this guy's kind of&nbsp;
dim sort of stuff where we said, you know,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:05.760 --> 00:04:11.920
a lot of these people really aren't thinking and&nbsp;
they are being manipulated by religious ideology.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:11.920 --> 00:04:17.200
They're being manipulated by misunderstandings&nbsp;
about not only cultural but also economic&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:17.200 --> 00:04:23.280
issues. The idea of education as right as&nbsp;
left wing indoctrination started to grow.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:23.920 --> 00:04:31.600
Then you get Obama and Obama as the first black&nbsp;
president. A half black man started to take&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:31.600 --> 00:04:36.640
the anti intellectualism and the radicalism and&nbsp;
the misunderstanding of economics and the lack&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:36.640 --> 00:04:45.520
of media literacy and coalesce it with racism&nbsp;
that still then was sort of beneath the surface,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:45.520 --> 00:04:50.160
although it was coming out the number&nbsp;
of death threats against Barack Obama,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:50.880 --> 00:04:54.880
birther ism, etc.. So by then it was it&nbsp;
was really starting to bubble up and look&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:54.880 --> 00:04:59.680
like the modern thing. And then comes Trump&nbsp;
and like, think the. At this point, right,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:00.560 --> 00:05:04.880
we go back to Roe v. Wade and the start of&nbsp;
radicalization of the more religious right,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:05.440 --> 00:05:10.400
you go to the civil rights era and you start&nbsp;
with the radicalization of the more overtly&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:10.400 --> 00:05:17.520
racist you get to the 80s and you add unthinking&nbsp;
Reaganomics. And then Newt Gingrich comes in in 94&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:17.520 --> 00:05:22.240
and gets us the next step. And then Bush and then&nbsp;
the racism under Obama and then in comes Trump.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:22.800 --> 00:05:27.520
And Trump really doesn't have to do much at&nbsp;
that point other than to make people feel&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:27.520 --> 00:05:34.480
good or comfortable going public with all of this&nbsp;
stuff that has built up comes down the escalator,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:34.480 --> 00:05:40.080
as he says, and talks about Mexican rapists and&nbsp;
some, I'm sure, good people as whole countries&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:40.640 --> 00:05:44.960
making fun of a, you know, person with a&nbsp;
disability, all of this different stuff,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:45.600 --> 00:05:51.840
and then it just explodes. So it's actually&nbsp;
notable. And I encourage you to read the the piece&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:51.840 --> 00:05:57.760
and by James Rosen in The Hartford Courant. It's&nbsp;
actually notable when you look at it how little&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:57.760 --> 00:06:05.120
Trump had to do with the radicalization itself,&nbsp;
but how perfectly timed he was to just drop in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:05.120 --> 00:06:09.200
and coalesce it. Now, the Republican Party,&nbsp;
remember, didn't want Trump at the beginning.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:10.080 --> 00:06:16.000
They wanted they wanted the radicalization, but&nbsp;
they wanted it to happen with someone that's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:16.000 --> 00:06:22.960
more polished, like a Mitt Romney. Remember the&nbsp;
evangelical grifter's who in 2012 were saying&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:22.960 --> 00:06:28.800
Romney's the chosen guy? Some of them ended up&nbsp;
like Mike. Mike Lendell from my pillow believe&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:28.800 --> 00:06:34.400
Trump is the chosen by God guy. So the Republicans&nbsp;
did want this type of radicalization. They believe&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:34.400 --> 00:06:37.920
it's good for fundraising. They believe it's good&nbsp;
electorally, but they wanted it with someone more&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:37.920 --> 00:06:42.160
polished, like a Mitt Romney. We kind of forget&nbsp;
about that because Romney and Romney lost and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:42.160 --> 00:06:48.160
Trump Trump won. And early on, the Republican&nbsp;
Party didn't want Trump. Eventually, he was&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:48.160 --> 00:06:53.520
able to commandeer the party. He said a lot of the&nbsp;
quiet parts that were sort of quiet under Obama,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:54.240 --> 00:07:00.160
although increasingly less so out loud, and&nbsp;
the establishment people early thought Trump&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:00.160 --> 00:07:05.200
was destructive to the party. Trump was bad to&nbsp;
the party. But they eventually got you know,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:05.200 --> 00:07:08.960
they acquiesce now. They were right that it was&nbsp;
bad because ultimately the Republicans lost the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:08.960 --> 00:07:13.680
House in twenty eighteen and the Senate in twenty&nbsp;
twenty and the White House in twenty twenty. But&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:13.680 --> 00:07:20.640
the main takeaway is that Republicans&nbsp;
embraced extremism well before Donald Trump.

